       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 20 mg is above the FDA-labeled maximum for amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Doses above 10 mg raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia, and they usually add little blood pressure benefit.\n\nHere are some options, depending on what you're trying to do:\n\n- **Standard dosing:** Start at 5 mg daily, or 2.5 mg in frail patients. Titrate to a maximum of 10 mg daily.\n- **Already on 10 mg and still not at goal:** Adding a second agent is usually better than going higher. Common choices are an ACE inhibitor or ARB, or a thiazide-like diuretic.\n- **Possible typo:** If you meant 10 mg or 2.5 mg, tell me and I'll enter it.\n\nPlease also check the order with your resident or attending, and with pharmacy if needed. If there's a specific reason for 20 mg, tell me and your supervising physician can confirm it. I can enter the order once you give me a corrected dose or confirm the intent."
      }
     ],
